# Keep Documentation Current

Whenever this project is updated, update the relevant documentation in the same change.

## What to update

Match docs to the change:

| Kind of change | Typical docs |
| --- | --- |
| Features, limitations, roadmap | `README.md`, `docs/design.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/deep_features.md` |
| SQL syntax or semantics | `docs/sql.md`, examples under `examples/` when user-facing |
| Tests, coverage, quality gates | `docs/testing.md`, `README.md` test/coverage counts when they change |
| Benchmarks or perf claims | `docs/benchmarks.md` |
| Demo / wedge packaging | `docs/cte_index_wedge.md`, related comparison docs |

## Rules

- Do not ship behavior, API, or roadmap changes with stale docs that still describe the old design.
- Prefer editing existing docs over adding new markdown files unless a new topic needs its own page.
- Keep counts and status lines honest (test counts, “next step”, known limitations).
- Documentation updates belong in the same commit as the code when practical.
